<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bruges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brugge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bruges is a city in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> by canals. From the 12th to the 15th century it was the commercial capital of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, home of a Hanseatic office and Italian banking houses, a leading centre of the Flemish cloth trade and of painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-eyck">Jan van Eyck</a>. The silting of its waterway shifted trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, leaving a remarkably preserved medieval centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bruges.jpg" alt="Bruges" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Br%C3%BCgge_Blick_vom_Belfried_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
